1
00:00:00,165 --> 00:00:01,855
<u>Data Storage & Consistency</u>

2
00:00:03,185 --> 00:00:04,515
<i>How should storage be selected?</i>

3
00:00:05,770 --> 00:00:11,200
Start from data ownership, access patterns, transactions, consistency needs, scale, failure behavior, and cost.

4
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:15,280
“SQL vs NoSQL” is a requirements decision, not an ideology.

5
00:00:16,785 --> 00:00:18,475
<i>When is relational storage attractive?</i>

6
00:00:19,990 --> 00:00:23,470
Relational storage is attractive when relationships, structured queries, constraints,

7
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,210
and multi-record transactions matter.

8
00:00:25,910 --> 00:00:29,430
A relational database can also scale; it is not inherently a small-system choice.

9
00:00:30,961 --> 00:00:32,901
<i>When can non-relational storage be useful?</i>

10
00:00:34,435 --> 00:00:38,795
Document, key-value, or wide-column storage can fit simple known access patterns, flexible schemas,

11
00:00:38,925 --> 00:00:42,095
specialized workloads, or designs where horizontal partitioning is central.

12
00:00:43,625 --> 00:00:44,565
<i>What is an index?</i>

13
00:00:46,091 --> 00:00:49,001
An index is an additional data structure that speeds selected reads.

14
00:00:49,701 --> 00:00:53,671
It consumes storage and makes writes more expensive, so indexes should follow real query patterns.

15
00:00:55,185 --> 00:00:56,165
<i>What is replication?</i>

16
00:00:57,670 --> 00:01:01,630
Replication maintains copies of data for redundancy, locality, or read capacity.

17
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:05,380
Replicas can lag behind the primary, so copied data may be stale.

18
00:01:06,889 --> 00:01:07,959
<i>What is a read replica?</i>

19
00:01:09,499 --> 00:01:12,749
A read replica serves reads from a replicated copy instead of the primary.

20
00:01:13,459 --> 00:01:18,129
It can reduce primary read load, but replication lag may violate immediate read-after-write expectations.

21
00:01:19,649 --> 00:01:21,099
<i>What is partitioning or sharding?</i>

22
00:01:22,614 --> 00:01:26,234
Partitioning, or sharding, divides data across nodes, usually by a key.

23
00:01:26,944 --> 00:01:31,614
A poor partition key can create hot partitions, uneven storage, and expensive cross-partition operations.

24
00:01:33,489 --> 00:01:34,539
<i>What is a transaction?</i>

25
00:01:35,786 --> 00:01:39,476
A transaction groups operations under defined atomicity and isolation guarantees.

26
00:01:40,176 --> 00:01:42,686
It is used to protect invariants that must change together.

27
00:01:44,209 --> 00:01:45,139
<i>What is durability?</i>

28
00:01:46,675 --> 00:01:51,225
Durability asks whether acknowledged data survives failures such as process, node, or regional loss.

29
00:01:51,935 --> 00:01:55,295
The guarantee depends on logging, replica acknowledgements, and the failure model.

30
00:01:56,801 --> 00:01:58,101
<i>What is strong consistency?</i>

31
00:01:59,622 --> 00:02:04,382
Strong consistency is an umbrella term for guarantees that prevent clients from observing arbitrarily stale state.

32
00:02:05,082 --> 00:02:09,202
When precision matters, name the exact guarantee, such as linearizable reads, read-after-write,

33
00:02:09,362 --> 00:02:10,622
or session-level consistency.

34
00:02:12,158 --> 00:02:13,578
<i>What is eventual consistency?</i>

35
00:02:15,099 --> 00:02:20,769
Eventual consistency allows replicas to temporarily differ but requires them to converge when updates stop and delivery succeeds.

36
00:02:21,459 --> 00:02:23,379
It does not mean random or undefined data.

37
00:02:24,894 --> 00:02:26,554
<i>What is read-after-write consistency?</i>

38
00:02:28,430 --> 00:02:33,240
Read-after-write consistency means a client can observe its completed write in subsequent reads according to the stated scope.

39
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:37,090
This is often important for user-visible updates such as profile changes.

40
00:02:38,354 --> 00:02:40,134
<i>What does CAP theorem actually say?</i>

41
00:02:41,651 --> 00:02:45,071
CAP stands for Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance.

42
00:02:45,771 --> 00:02:49,561
During a network partition, an affected operation must trade consistency against availability;

43
00:02:49,761 --> 00:02:52,511
CAP is not a generic rule to “choose any two” for the whole system.
